\name{faq-common-names}
\alias{faq-common-names}
\title{FAQ - How are common names determined?}
\description{
When a function builds one result from several inputs, the inputs must
agree on their names. If they don’t, the function errors instead of
silently picking one input’s names.

These functions follow the rules:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=rray_add]{rray_add()}}, \code{\link[=rray_subtract]{rray_subtract()}},
\code{\link[=rray_multiply]{rray_multiply()}},
\code{\link[=rray_divide]{rray_divide()}}, and
\code{\link[=rray_exponentiate]{rray_exponentiate()}}
\item \code{\link[=rray_and]{rray_and()}}, \code{\link[=rray_or]{rray_or()}}, and
\code{\link[=rray_xor]{rray_xor()}}
\item \code{\link[=rray_pmax]{rray_pmax()}} and \code{\link[=rray_pmin]{rray_pmin()}}
\item \code{\link[=rray_combine]{rray_combine()}} and \code{\link[=rray_stack]{rray_stack()}}
}
}
\section{Rules}{
For each axis of the result:

All inputs with names along that axis must have exactly the same names.
If all names are the same, the result has those names. If no input is
named, the result is not named. If any names are different, an error is
thrown to avoid arbitrarily picking between the inputs. If an input is
broadcast or has no names, it is excluded from common name
determination.

To resolve a conflict, preemptively remove the names from one input with
\code{\link[=rray_set_names]{rray_set_names()}}.
}

\section{Examples}{
Matching names are kept, and different names are an error:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{x <- array(1:2, 2, dimnames = list(c("r1", "r2")))
y <- array(1:2, 2, dimnames = list(c("a", "b")))

rray_add(x, x)
#> r1 r2 
#>  2  4

rray_add(x, y)
#> Error in `rray_add()`:
#> ! Can't find common names at axis 1. `x` and `y` have different names at position 1: "r1" and "a".

rray_add(x, rray_set_names(y, NULL))
#> r1 r2 
#>  2  4
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

An input without names on an axis doesn’t participate there. Here, each
input names a different axis, so both sets of names are kept:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{x <- array(1:4, c(2, 2), dimnames = list(c("r1", "r2"), NULL))
y <- array(5:8, c(2, 2), dimnames = list(NULL, c("c1", "c2")))

rray_add(x, y)
#>    c1 c2
#> r1  6 10
#> r2  8 12
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A broadcast axis doesn’t participate either. \code{"total"} names a single
row that gets repeated, so it no longer describes the result and is
dropped:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{x <- array(1:3, c(1, 3), dimnames = list("total", c("a", "b", "c")))
y <- array(1:6, c(2, 3), dimnames = list(c("r1", "r2"), c("a", "b", "c")))

rray_multiply(x, y)
#>    a b  c
#> r1 1 6 15
#> r2 2 8 18
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Empty names count, so \code{c("r1", "")} and \code{c("r1", "r2")} are different:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{x <- array(1:2, 2, dimnames = list(c("r1", "")))
y <- array(1:2, 2, dimnames = list(c("r1", "r2")))

rray_add(x, y)
#> Error in `rray_add()`:
#> ! Can't find common names at axis 1. `x` and `y` have different names at position 2: "" and "r2".
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Combining and stacking}{
\code{\link[=rray_combine]{rray_combine()}} never checks names on \code{.axis}. They
are combined from every input, with \code{""} for inputs that have none.
Every other axis follows the rules above:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{a <- array(1:2, c(1, 2), dimnames = list("r1", c("x", "y")))
b <- array(3:4, c(1, 2))
c <- array(5:6, c(1, 2), dimnames = list("r3", c("x", "z")))

rray_combine(a, b, c, .axis = 1)
#> Error in `rray_combine()`:
#> ! Can't find common names at axis 2. `..1` and `..3` have different names at position 2: "y" and "z".

c <- array(5:6, c(1, 2), dimnames = list("r3", c("x", "y")))

rray_combine(a, b, c, .axis = 1)
#>    x y
#> r1 1 2
#>    3 4
#> r3 5 6
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{\link[=rray_stack]{rray_stack()}} names the new axis with the names of
\code{...}. Every other axis follows the rules above, which catches data that
was reordered:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{jan <- array(1:2, 2, dimnames = list(c("north", "south")))
feb <- array(3:4, 2, dimnames = list(c("south", "north")))

rray_stack(jan = jan, feb = feb, .axis = 2)
#> Error in `rray_stack()`:
#> ! Can't find common names at axis 1. `jan` and `feb` have different names at position 1: "north" and "south".
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Comparisons}{
Comparison functions like \code{\link[=rray_equal]{rray_equal()}} and
\code{\link[=rray_greater_than]{rray_greater_than()}} return a logical relation
about their inputs rather than a new value of the input type, so they
don’t return names on the result and aren’t subject to these checks:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{x <- array(c(TRUE, FALSE), 2, dimnames = list(c("r1", "r2")))
y <- array(c(TRUE, TRUE), 2, dimnames = list(c("a", "b")))

rray_equal(x, y)
#> [1]  TRUE FALSE
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

